// This port's own room-106 icon-grid dump (notes section 135) shows plain floor icon 173 there.
#define BOSS3_ENDING_FLOOR_ICON 173
// Real: `while(timer_cnt<180) rotate_pal();` -- ~1.5s blocking pause before the first explosion.
// Ported as a plain per-tick countdown, not a real-time ms conversion -- see s_boss3_ending_active's
// own comment for why (this animation's own poll function, like real endgame_movement(), IS the
// per-tick call site already).
#define BOSS3_ENDING_PRELUDE_TICKS 180
// Real: `actor[34].i2=6;` -- ticks between one explosion and the next, reloaded after each.
#define BOSS3_ENDING_EXPLODE_TICKS 6
// How many s_sprites[] slots this port reserves to stand in for real code's one reusable actor[34]
// flame-visual slot -- see s_boss3_ending_fx_index's own comment (in got_boss3_ending_start()) for why
// more than one is needed here, unlike real code's single hardcoded slot.
#define BOSS3_ENDING_FX_SLOTS 3
// Deliberate deviation from s_explosion_template's own real per-frame hold (ACTOR107's own real
// `speed` field is 6 ticks/frame -- confirmed by direct resource decode -- the same value
// got_boss1_segment_explode()'s other, already-working use of this template runs at for a single,
// unhurried 4x4 boss-segment death). wootbeer (play-test round 3): "the explosion animation plays now on
// the house but it goes so fast I can only see maybe the first frame of the animation for each
// explosion." Root cause isn't a rendering bug -- at the template's own real speed, each flame DOES
// keep animating correctly across several ticks -- but with a brand new explosion (and flame) landing
// on a completely different tile every BOSS3_ENDING_EXPLODE_TICKS (6) ticks, a viewer's attention
// jumps to each new flash faster than the template's own comparatively slow, single-segment-death
// pacing was ever tuned for. Explicitly overriding the copied template's own `speed`/`speed_tick`
// (see got_boss3_ending_poll()'s own spawn site) to this much shorter per-frame hold instead lets each
// individual burst's own real 4-frame cycle (ACTOR107's own real `frame_sequence`, {0,1,2,3} --
// confirmed by decode, no repeated frames) complete well within that same 6-tick gap, so each flash
// actually reads as a small multi-frame burst rather than a single freeze-frame before the next one
// steals focus -- same class of wootbeer-driven, playtest-tuned deviation from a shared template's own raw
// resource values already established by got_actor_destroyed()'s own num_shots history.
#define BOSS3_ENDING_FX_FRAME_TICKS 1

